Stop the empty KV binding locking the worker directory

`npx wrangler kv namespace create CHALLENGES` cannot be run from `worker/`,
which is a problem, because that is the directory the instruction to run it is
written in.

```
✘ [ERROR] Processing wrangler.jsonc configuration:
    - "kv_namespaces[0]" bindings should have a string "id" field but got
      {"binding":"CHALLENGES","id":""}.
```

Wrangler validates the whole config before it runs **any** command. So a
placeholder entry does not describe work still to do — it locks the directory,
and the first thing it refuses is the command that would produce the missing
value. A perfect deadlock, shipped, with the way out written directly above it.

## The fix

Remove the block. The binding is now **absent** rather than present-and-empty,
and the comment explains why absent is the correct shipped state instead of
reading as an oversight the next person should tidy up by re-adding a
placeholder.

Absent is safe, and that is not an assumption — `src/index.js:102` already
handles it:

```js
if (!env.CHALLENGES) missing.push('CHALLENGES (the KV namespace binding)');
```

A missing binding is reported as a configuration problem and the broker answers
`unconfigured`, exactly as it already does for `RP_ID`, `ORIGINS`, the R2
values and the GitHub App. Every other unset thing in this worker degrades that
way; the KV binding was the one that took the directory down with it.

## Also updated, so the instruction is the same in all three places

- **`worker/wrangler.jsonc`** — the block is gone, replaced by the reason it is
  gone and the shape to paste back once you have an id.
- **`worker/README.md`** — the bindings table said the binding exists.
- **`PAYMENTS-CHECKLIST.md`** — item 1.1 said the id goes into
  `kv_namespaces[0].id`, which no longer exists to put it in.

## The id is not a secret

Worth saying, because it looks like one and the instinct will be to hide it. A
namespace id names a namespace. It grants nothing — reaching the data still
requires a token bound to the account. Commit it like any other config.

## Verified

- `worker/wrangler.jsonc` parses with comments stripped; `kv_namespaces` is
  absent and `name` is still `fcpm-broker`.
- Broker tests: **119 passing**, unchanged. They inject `env` directly, so they
  never depended on the declaration.

## What this does not do

It does not create the namespace — that needs your account. After this,
`cd worker && npx wrangler kv namespace create CHALLENGES` will actually run.
